his to our universe: if the universe is a 3D closed manifold (a 3-sphere), it does not require a 4D embedding space to exist. Its intrinsic curvature is all that matters. The "centre" in the embedding space is not a physical location in our universe. So the universe can be perfectly well-defined without a centre, even if it's closed. ### Summary - The circle's centre exists in the plane, but the plane isn't part of the circle's 1D world. - If the universe is analogous to the circle's surface, the centre would exist only if there's a real higher-dimensional space — which we have no evidence for and which standard cosmology doesn't require. - In intrinsic geometry (the kind physics uses), there is no centre. The "central point" is an artifact of picturing the sphere as embedded. So, to directly answer your question: Yes, if our universe were embedded in a real 4D space, that space would have a centre point. But that centre would not be part of our universe, and its existence would depend on an unproven assumption. Within the universe we actually measure and live in, there is no centre — not even a hidden one — because the geometry is self-contained and needs no outside. The circle is all there is, and the point at its middle is just a ghost from our perspective, not a real place.
